   Misunderstanding. I see now that you added a comment in dog.c where
dog_eat() might return 2; but as I said, that only happens in rare
cases involving genocide --- it has nothing to do with choking, unless
I'm mistaken.
   The rn2(4) chance of feeling guilty seems gratuitous; surely either
you should /always/ get punished for killing your pet, or you should
/never/ get punished, since it wasn't really your fault? I'm not sure
whether the current behavior is intentional, or an oversight by the
DevTeam... but I certainly don't think it should be random!

   There is a typo in could_use_item() --- you have

     if (otmp->otyp == otmp2->otyp ||
         (otmp->otyp == FOOD_CLASS && otmp2->otyp == FOOD_CLASS))
         return TRUE;

instead of the intended

     if (otmp->otyp == otmp2->otyp ||
         (otmp->oclass == FOOD_CLASS && otmp2->oclass == FOOD_CLASS))
         return TRUE;

(although really some foods are handy to have around, such as carrots
and wolfsbane --- although if the pet isn't going to use them for their
special purposes, I guess there's no reason to hang onto them.)

   castmm() and ucast_wizard_spell() call the function "snprintf()",
which doesn't exist on most platforms. You should be using sprintf()
with an appropriately sized buffer. (The only thing snprintf() does
differently is truncate the output if it runs long, and Nethack players
won't tolerate truncated output in the first place!)

